Aim
To create a secure VPC architecture using a Bastion Server and NAT Gateway for accessing private EC2 instances and enabling internet connectivity for instances in private subnets.

Procedure
Part A — VPC Creation and Subnet Design
Step 1: Create VPC


Login to Amazon Web Services AWS Management Console.


Go to:


Services → VPC




Click Create VPC.


Select:


VPC only




Configure:


Name:
Custom-VPC


IPv4 CIDR:
10.0.0.0/16




Click Create VPC.



Step 2: Create Public and Private Subnets
Public Subnet (Bastion)


Go to:


Subnets → Create Subnet




Configure:


Name:
Public-Subnet


CIDR:
10.0.1.0/24




Select same Availability Zone.


Create subnet.



Private Subnet (Database Server)


Create another subnet.


Configure:


Name:
Private-Subnet


CIDR:
10.0.2.0/24




Create subnet.



Step 3: Create Internet Gateway


Open:


Internet Gateways




Click:


Create Internet Gateway




Name:
Custom-IGW


Create IGW.


Attach it to Custom-VPC.



Step 4: Configure Route Tables
Public Route Table


Create Route Table:
Public-RT


Add Route:


DestinationTarget0.0.0.0/0Internet Gateway


Associate with:


Public Subnet





Private Route Table


Create Route Table:
Private-RT


Associate with:


Private Subnet




Keep only local route initially.



Part B — Configure Security Groups
Step 5: Bastion Server Security Group
Create Security Group:
RulePortSourceSSH22My IP
Attach to Bastion Server.

Step 6: Database Server Security Group
Create another Security Group:
RulePortSourceMySQL/Aurora330610.0.1.0/24SSH22Bastion Private IP
Do NOT allow public access.

Part C — Launch EC2 Instances
Step 7: Launch Bastion Server


Launch EC2 Instance.


Configure:


Name:
Bastion-Server


AMI: Ubuntu


Subnet: Public Subnet


Auto Assign Public IP: Enabled




Select/Create key pair:
bastion.pem


Attach Bastion Security Group.


Launch instance.



Step 8: Launch Database Server


Launch another EC2 instance.


Configure:


Name:
DB-Server


AMI: Ubuntu


Subnet: Private Subnet


No Public IP




Select key pair:
dbserver.pem


Attach Database Security Group.


Launch instance.



Part D — Access Private EC2 Through Bastion Server
Step 9: Copy DB Key to Bastion Server
From local PowerShell:
scp -i bastion.pem dbserver.pem ubuntu@<BASTION_PUBLIC_IP>:/home/ubuntu/

Step 10: Connect to Bastion Server
Run:
ssh -i bastion.pem ubuntu@<BASTION_PUBLIC_IP>
Verify files:
ls
Output:
bastion.pemdbserver.pem

Step 11: Connect from Bastion to Database Server
From Bastion terminal:
ssh -i dbserver.pem ubuntu@10.0.2.x
Connection to private database server becomes successful.

Part E — Configure NAT Gateway
Step 12: Create NAT Gateway


Go to:


NAT Gateways




Click:


Create NAT Gateway




Configure:


Name:
Custom-NAT


Subnet:


Public Subnet






Allocate Elastic IP.


Click Create NAT Gateway.



Step 13: Configure Route Table for NAT


Go to:


Route Tables




Select:


Private-RT




Click:


Routes → Edit Routes




Add Route:


DestinationTarget0.0.0.0/0NAT Gateway


Save changes.



Step 14: Verify Internet Access in Private EC2
Connect to private EC2 through Bastion and run:
sudo apt update
or
ping google.com
Internet access should now work successfully.

Observation


VPC with public and private subnets created successfully.


Bastion server accessible from local machine.


Private EC2 instance securely accessed through Bastion server.


NAT Gateway enabled outbound internet access for private subnet instances.


Database server remained inaccessible directly from the internet.



Result
Successfully created a secure VPC architecture using a Bastion Server and NAT Gateway, enabling secure SSH access to private EC2 instances and controlled internet connectivity for private subnet resources.
